    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3c5b0958-9780-524f-adda-08c7d0bfb03d",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/37e24c205cc1d8a9c4db50f1bd01ce95680a1c3db179b58b561cb5a94fed45af.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-2$ is a simple root.\n- **II.** $x=5$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n- **III.** $x=5$ is a simple root.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=-2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=5$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=5$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=5$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
