  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186129154,
      "id": "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443",
      "title": "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a66bb003-1879-5b27-8b63-9a7ab19dfc85",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/df8d7272d25b963997a4e6f8c7fd15e4c576ef90984552bb649d5b66d3529185.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are correct?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n- **II.** $x=1$ is a simple root.\n- **III.** $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=2$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4ca4329d-dd63-51de-bdb6-56a88c0330d4",
